  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "$$\n{1}_{A}(c)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(c)=1\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=1$\n- ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0$\n- $c\\in A\\cap B$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{\u2015}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{\u2015}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c) & =min{{1}_{A}(c),{1}_{B}(c)} \\\\  & =min{0,1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(c)=0,$ we conclude that $c$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
